/* ==========================================================================
   GLOSSARY — terms and acronyms from the SSDSI Lean Six Sigma Black Belt course.
   t = term, x = what the acronym stands for (omit if not an acronym),
   p = phase, d = the explanation shown when the row is expanded.

   Sources: the Green Belt transcripts (Modules #0-#15) and the standalone
   Black Belt transcripts (Modules #1-#15), plus standard Lean Six Sigma usage
   where the course assumes a term without defining it.
   ========================================================================== */

window.ICBB_GLOSSARY = [

/* ---------- FOUNDATIONS ---------- */
{t:"Six Sigma", p:"Foundations", d:"A structured method for reducing variation and defects in a process, using data rather than opinion to decide what to change. The name comes from the statistical goal of fitting six standard deviations between the process average and the nearest specification limit — which works out to about 3.4 defects per million opportunities. In practice it is less about hitting that exact number and more about the discipline: measure before you act, find the real causes, prove the fix worked."},
{t:"Lean", p:"Foundations", d:"A method for removing waste from a process so that work flows without interruption. It came out of Toyota's production system and focuses on anything the customer would not willingly pay for: waiting, moving things around, storing inventory, reworking mistakes. Where Six Sigma attacks variation, Lean attacks delay. Most real projects need both."},
{t:"LSS", x:"Lean Six Sigma", p:"Foundations", d:"The combination of the two methods. Lean removes the waste and speeds up flow; Six Sigma reduces variation and defects. Used together because a fast process that produces defects is no better than a slow accurate one. The DMAIC framework is the container that holds both sets of tools."},
{t:"DMAIC", x:"Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control", p:"Foundations", d:"The five-phase problem-solving sequence at the heart of Six Sigma. Define scopes the problem, Measure establishes the baseline, Analyze finds the causes, Improve tests and implements the fix, Control makes it stick. The order matters — the most common project failure is jumping to a solution before Analyze has produced evidence for it."},
{t:"Y = f(x)", p:"Foundations", d:"The core equation of Six Sigma, read as 'Y is a function of x'. Y is the outcome you care about — cycle time, defect rate, cost. The x's are the inputs that drive it. The whole method rests on the belief that if you find and control the vital few x's, the Y takes care of itself. It is why you never work directly on the Y."},
{t:"VOC", x:"Voice of the Customer", p:"Foundations", d:"What the customer actually needs and expects, gathered directly rather than assumed. Surveys, complaints, interviews, returns data. VOC is the raw material you translate into CTQs — measurable requirements. A project that skips VOC often improves something no customer cares about."},
{t:"CTQ", x:"Critical to Quality", p:"Foundations", d:"A specific, measurable characteristic that matters to the customer. The bridge between a vague customer wish and something you can put a number on. 'Fast service' is not a CTQ; 'order confirmed within 4 hours' is. Every CTQ needs a target and a tolerance."},
{t:"COPQ", x:"Cost of Poor Quality", p:"Foundations", d:"The money the organisation loses because the process does not run correctly. Splits into four buckets: prevention, appraisal, internal failure (caught before the customer sees it) and external failure (the customer feels it). External failure is by far the most expensive, which is why lost customers make a stronger business case than staff rework hours."},
{t:"VA", x:"Value Added", p:"Foundations", d:"Work the customer would willingly pay for, that transforms the product or service, and that is done right the first time. All three conditions must hold. In most processes VA is a surprisingly small fraction of total time — often under 5%."},
{t:"NVA", x:"Non-Value Added", p:"Foundations", d:"Everything else: waiting, moving, storing, inspecting, correcting. Some NVA is necessary (regulatory checks, for instance) and gets called business-value-added, but it is still not something the customer wants to pay for. The goal is to eliminate it, not to do it faster."},
{t:"Muda", p:"Foundations", d:"The Japanese term for waste — the NVA activity Lean targets. One of three related concepts alongside Mura and Muri. Muda is what the seven wastes catalogue."},
{t:"Seven Wastes", p:"Foundations", d:"The standard catalogue of Muda: transport, inventory, motion, waiting, overproduction, overprocessing and defects. Often remembered as TIMWOOD. Some versions add an eighth for unused human talent. Useful as a checklist when walking a process — each waste points to a different kind of fix."},
{t:"5S", p:"Foundations", d:"A workplace organisation method: Sort, Set in order, Shine, Standardise, Sustain. Removes what is not needed, gives everything a fixed place, keeps it clean, writes down the standard, then audits it. Looks trivial and is often the cheapest improvement available, because it eliminates searching and makes abnormalities visible instantly."},
{t:"Poka-Yoke", p:"Foundations", d:"Error-proofing — designing the process so the mistake becomes impossible or is caught immediately. A form that will not submit with a blank required field, a connector that only fits one way. Stronger than training or reminders, because it does not depend on anyone remembering."},
{t:"Kanban", p:"Foundations", d:"A signalling system that triggers work only when downstream demand exists. Physically a card, bin or board slot; the principle is that nothing is produced until something is consumed. The mechanism that makes a pull system work in practice."},
{t:"TAKT Time", p:"Foundations", d:"The pace at which the process must produce to meet demand, calculated as available time divided by customer demand. If you have 480 minutes and need 12 units, TAKT is 40 minutes per unit. It is a requirement, not a measurement — you compare your actual cycle times against it to see whether the process can keep up."},
{t:"Cycle Time", p:"Foundations", d:"How long one step takes to complete one unit. Distinct from lead time, which covers the whole process including all the waiting between steps. A process can have short cycle times at every station and still have a terrible lead time, which is exactly what batching produces."},
{t:"Lead Time", p:"Foundations", d:"Total elapsed time from the start of the process to delivery, including every wait, queue and handoff. This is what the customer experiences. Reducing it usually means removing delay between steps rather than speeding up the steps themselves."},
{t:"PCT", x:"Process Cycle Time", p:"Foundations", d:"The total time through the process, value-added and non-value-added together. Used as the denominator when calculating process cycle efficiency. In a batch process this figure is often dominated by storage and queueing rather than by work."},
{t:"VAT", x:"Value Added Time", p:"Foundations", d:"The sum of the value-added steps only. Typically a small fraction of PCT. Comparing the two is the single most persuasive number a value stream map produces."},
{t:"PCE", x:"Process Cycle Efficiency", p:"Foundations", d:"Value-added time divided by total process cycle time, expressed as a percentage. Below 5% is common in office and service processes and is not unusual in manufacturing either. Anything above 25% is generally considered lean."},
{t:"Single-Piece Flow", p:"Foundations", d:"Moving one unit at a time through the process rather than batches. Eliminates the waiting that batching creates, where every unit sits until the whole batch is ready to move. Usually collapses lead time dramatically without any change to the work itself."},
{t:"Pull System", p:"Foundations", d:"Work is released only when the downstream step signals it is ready. Prevents inventory accumulating between steps and exposes problems quickly, because there is no buffer hiding them. Kanban is the usual implementation."},
{t:"Swim Lane Map", p:"Foundations", d:"A process map with horizontal lanes, one per person, role or department. Makes handoffs between groups visible, which is where delay and dropped work tend to live. Required as a project deliverable in this course."},
{t:"VSM", x:"Value Stream Map", p:"Foundations", d:"A diagram of the whole flow of material and information needed to deliver a product or service, annotated with cycle times, wait times and inventory. Unlike a process map it shows time explicitly, which is what makes the waste quantifiable."},
{t:"CSVSM", x:"Current State Value Stream Map", p:"Foundations", d:"The value stream map of the process as it runs today, warts included. Drawn from direct observation rather than from how the process is supposed to work. The baseline everything else is measured against."},
{t:"FSVSM", x:"Future State Value Stream Map", p:"Foundations", d:"The value stream map of the process as you intend it to run after improvement. Built by applying flow, pull and levelling to the current state and recalculating the times. The gap between the two maps is the project's business case."},
{t:"Tollgate", p:"Foundations", d:"A formal review at the end of each DMAIC phase where the work is checked before the project proceeds. In this course each tollgate is submitted to SSDSI and must be approved before the next phase begins — five separate review cycles."},
{t:"VOB", x:"Voice of the Business", p:"Foundations", d:"What the organisation needs from the process — margin, compliance, capacity, risk. Sits alongside VOC and sometimes conflicts with it. The customer might want same-day turnaround; the business needs it to be profitable. Charters that acknowledge both are more defensible."},
{t:"VOE", x:"Voice of the Employee", p:"Foundations", d:"What the people doing the work say about it. Frequently the fastest route to the real x's, because the operators already know where the process breaks down. Formalised later through Gemba walks and process mapping, but worth collecting early."},
{t:"Mura", p:"Foundations", d:"Unevenness in a process — the peaks and troughs in workload that force you to staff for the peak and idle through the trough. Levelling demand often removes waste that no amount of local efficiency improvement would touch."},
{t:"Muri", p:"Foundations", d:"Overburden — pushing people or equipment beyond reasonable capacity. Produces errors, breakdowns and burnout, and is usually a symptom of Mura upstream."},
{t:"Kaizen", p:"Foundations", d:"Continuous incremental improvement, usually driven by the people doing the work rather than by a formal project. Also used for short focused improvement events lasting a few days. Complements DMAIC rather than replacing it — Kaizen handles the obvious, DMAIC handles the problems whose causes are unknown."},
{t:"Gemba", p:"Foundations", d:"The actual place where work happens. A Gemba walk means going to observe the process directly rather than relying on reports or descriptions. Almost always reveals steps that no documented procedure mentions."},
{t:"WIP", x:"Work in Process", p:"Foundations", d:"Units that have entered the process but not yet left it. High WIP means long lead times — Little's Law says lead time equals WIP divided by throughput. Capping WIP is one of the most reliable ways to shorten lead time without doing anything faster."},
{t:"Push System", p:"Foundations", d:"Work is released into the process according to a schedule or forecast, regardless of whether the next step is ready for it. Produces inventory pile-ups between steps. Drawn as a striped arrow on a value stream map."},
{t:"Standard Work", p:"Foundations", d:"The documented best-known method for performing a task, including sequence, timing and quality checks. Not bureaucracy for its own sake — without a standard there is no baseline to improve from, and any improvement quietly reverts."},
{t:"Visual Management", p:"Foundations", d:"Making process status visible at a glance, so problems are obvious without anyone having to ask. Floor markings, status boards, andon lights, colour coding. The fifth S depends on it."},
{t:"Spaghetti Diagram", p:"Foundations", d:"A drawing of the physical path a person or item travels through a workspace, traced onto a floor plan. The tangle that usually results makes excess motion and transport impossible to ignore. Cheap to produce and often instantly persuasive."},
{t:"Champion", p:"Foundations", d:"The senior sponsor who authorises the project, removes obstacles and owns the business result. Distinct from the process owner. A project without an engaged champion tends to stall the first time it needs someone's time."},
{t:"Process Owner", p:"Foundations", d:"The person accountable for the process day to day, who inherits the improvement once the project closes. Must be involved from Define, or the Control phase has nobody to hand over to."},
{t:"Belt Levels", p:"Foundations", d:"The certification hierarchy: Yellow Belt understands the basics and supports projects; Green Belt leads smaller projects alongside a day job; Black Belt leads complex projects full time and mentors Green Belts; Master Black Belt trains and coaches Black Belts and manages the deployment."},
{t:"TIMWOODS", p:"Foundations", d:"The course's acronym for the eight wastes: Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Overprocessing, Defects and Skills. Note the second S — SSDSI teaches eight wastes, not seven, with unused employee talent as the eighth."},
{t:"Waste of Skills", p:"Foundations", d:"The eighth waste: failing to use employees' full capability. Caused by weak performance management, incomplete skills assessment before hiring, or loading two people's work onto one. The fix is designing the process around cross-trained, flexible staff."},
{t:"Little's Law", p:"Foundations", d:"Lead time equals work in process divided by throughput. The equation that defines a queue. It means there are only two ways to shorten lead time: raise throughput, which usually costs money, or cut WIP, which usually means removing waste. The course illustrates it with the queue at Space Mountain."},
{t:"Throughput", p:"Foundations", d:"The number of units the process actually completes per unit of time. Set by the slowest step, never by the average — which is why adding capacity anywhere but the constraint changes nothing."},
{t:"Constraint", p:"Foundations", d:"The slowest step in a process, also called the bottleneck. It determines throughput for the whole line. Improving any other step just builds inventory in front of this one."},
{t:"JIT", x:"Just-In-Time", p:"Foundations", d:"Producing only what is ordered, exactly when needed, in the exact quantity, with minimal inventory at every stage. The course is emphatic that JIT should be the outcome of good flow and pull practice, not the starting point — implementing it first tends to break things."},
{t:"JIC", x:"Just-in-Case", p:"Foundations", d:"The opposite of JIT: holding inventory in case it is needed. The default state of most processes and a major source of the inventory waste Lean targets."},
{t:"Heijunka", p:"Foundations", d:"Levelling the workload so demand is spread evenly rather than arriving in peaks and troughs. One of the key elements of JIT alongside pull, kanban, takt, continuous flow and quick changeover."},
{t:"SMED", x:"Single-Minute Exchange of Die", p:"Foundations", d:"Quick changeover — reducing the time to switch a process from one product or task to another. Long changeovers are what make large batches look economical, so shortening them is often the precondition for single-piece flow."},
{t:"Pitch", p:"Foundations", d:"An alternative to takt time as the pacing signal, used where producing one unit at a time is impractical. Sets the rhythm at which a small fixed quantity moves through the process."},
{t:"Supermarket", p:"Foundations", d:"A controlled store of inventory held between processes, replenished only when drawn from. Named for the retail model: the customer takes from the shelf, and that act signals restocking. A standard element of the future state map."},
{t:"Ohno Circle", p:"Foundations", d:"Standing in one place on the process floor and simply watching for an extended period. Named after Taiichi Ohno, who used it to train managers to see waste. The course pairs it with the spaghetti diagram as a way to capture the current state."},
{t:"Line Balancing", p:"Foundations", d:"Distributing work across steps so each takes roughly the same time and none exceeds takt. The practical method is dividing each step's cycle time by takt time to get the minimum staffing that step needs, then comparing to actual staffing."},
{t:"Cellular Layout", p:"Foundations", d:"Arranging equipment and people by product flow rather than by function, so a unit moves through a compact cell instead of travelling between departments. The standard remedy for transport and motion waste."},
{t:"TPS", x:"Toyota Production System", p:"Foundations", d:"The post-war Toyota system from which Lean derives. 5S is one of its foundations. Worth knowing as the origin of nearly every Japanese term in the course."},
{t:"Visual Workplace", p:"Foundations", d:"A workplace where 5S has made the operating system visible, so it becomes self-explanatory, self-ordering and self-regulating. Employees can see the state of work without asking anyone."},
{t:"Abnormality Handling", p:"Foundations", d:"The documented answer to what happens when something goes wrong: who reports it, when, to whom, what corrective action follows, and when team members should step in to help. Without it, flow collapses the first time something unexpected happens."},
{t:"Red Tag", p:"Foundations", d:"A tag attached during the Sort phase of 5S to items that may not be needed, carrying the item name, location, date and who tagged it. Tagged items move to a holding area rather than straight to the bin, so removal decisions can be reviewed."},
{t:"Seiri", p:"Foundations", d:"Sort — the first S. Separate needed from unneeded items and dispose of the unneeded. The course's phrase is 'when in doubt, throw it out'. A first pass typically frees 30–70% of space."},
{t:"Seiton", p:"Foundations", d:"Set in Order — the second S. A place for everything and everything in its place, arranged by frequency of use. Tested by the three I's: identify the item, identify the location, identify the quantity allowed."},
{t:"Seiso", p:"Foundations", d:"Shine — the third S. Cleaning as inspection: a clean workplace exposes leaks, wear and misplaced items immediately. The course notes this is the stage where most 5S efforts fail."},
{t:"Seiketsu", p:"Foundations", d:"Standardise — the fourth S. Write down the standard for cleaning, labelling, replenishment and container formats, then audit against it. Without this the first three S's decay."},
{t:"Shitsuke", p:"Foundations", d:"Sustain — the fifth S. Maintaining the discipline through audits, training, recognition and fast intervention when standards slip. Requires management action, not just employee goodwill."},

/* ---------- DEFINE ---------- */
{t:"Project Charter", p:"Define", d:"The founding document of the project, containing the business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope, metrics, team and timeline. Signed by the champion. Its main function is to force agreement on what the problem is before anyone starts solving it."},
{t:"Business Case", p:"Define", d:"The section of the charter that says why the organisation should care, in money. Follows a fixed template: over a stated period, this metric was this bad against this target, and the gap costs this much. The number has to be traceable or it will not survive review."},
{t:"Problem Statement", p:"Define", d:"A description of what is wrong, where, when and by how much — and deliberately not why. Naming a cause here commits you to a conclusion before Analyze has produced any evidence, which is the most common charter error."},
{t:"Goal Statement", p:"Define", d:"What the project will achieve, by when, stated as a measurable change from baseline to target. Should include a guardrail so the goal cannot be met by damaging something else — a cycle time target with no quality guardrail invites corner-cutting."},
{t:"Scope", p:"Define", d:"The boundaries of the investigation: the first process task, the last process task, and what is explicitly in and out. In and out can include departments, personnel, sites and equipment. The out-of-scope list is what protects the project from expanding once it starts."},
{t:"Defect", p:"Define", d:"Any instance where the output fails to meet a customer requirement. One unit can carry several defects. Distinguishing defects from defectives matters because it changes which metrics and control charts apply."},
{t:"Defective", p:"Define", d:"A unit that contains at least one defect. A batch of 100 units with 150 defects might contain only 40 defectives. Counting the wrong one produces the wrong sigma level."},
{t:"DPU", x:"Defects Per Unit", p:"Define", d:"Total defects divided by total units. Simple and useful, but it ignores complexity — a unit with 50 opportunities and a unit with 3 are not comparable on DPU alone."},
{t:"DPMO", x:"Defects Per Million Opportunities", p:"Define", d:"DPO scaled to a million, which puts it on the scale used to look up sigma levels. Six Sigma performance is 3.4 DPMO, assuming the standard 1.5 sigma long-term shift."},
{t:"RTY", x:"Rolled Throughput Yield", p:"Define", d:"The probability a unit passes through every step of the process without any rework, calculated by multiplying the yields of each step. Brutally revealing — ten steps at 95% each gives an RTY of 60%. Exposes the hidden factory that step-level yields conceal."},
{t:"Sigma Level", p:"Define", d:"A single number summarising process capability, derived from DPMO. Higher is better: three sigma is roughly 66,800 DPMO, six sigma is 3.4. Convenient for communication, but it hides the shape of the distribution, so never rely on it alone."},
{t:"Pareto Chart", p:"Define", d:"A bar chart of causes ordered from most to least frequent, usually with a cumulative percentage line. Applies the 80:20 rule — a minority of causes generally accounts for the majority of defects. The standard tool for deciding where to aim."},
{t:"Unit", p:"Define", d:"The thing being produced or processed that you count — an application, a part, an invoice. Has to be defined precisely before any defect metric means anything."},
{t:"Opportunity", p:"Define", d:"Each chance for a defect to occur within one unit. A form with 12 required fields has 12 opportunities. The count is somewhat arbitrary, which is why sigma levels are only comparable when the opportunity definition is held constant."},
{t:"DPO", x:"Defects Per Opportunity", p:"Define", d:"Defects divided by the total number of opportunities. Normalises for complexity so processes of different sizes can be compared. Multiply by a million to get DPMO."},
{t:"FTY", x:"First Time Yield", p:"Define", d:"The proportion of units that pass a single step correctly the first time, before any rework. Multiply the FTYs of every step to get RTY."},

/* ---------- MEASURE ---------- */
{t:"SIPOC", x:"Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers", p:"Measure", d:"A one-page summary of the process at high level, built in a specific order: process first, then outputs, then requirements, then inputs. Sets the boundaries everyone agrees on before detailed mapping starts. The R in SIPOC(R) adds requirements for each output."},
{t:"Fishbone Diagram", p:"Measure", d:"Also called Ishikawa or cause-and-effect diagram. The effect goes at the head, and possible causes branch off by category — commonly People, Method, Machine, Material, Measurement and Environment. A structured brainstorm, not evidence; everything on it is a hypothesis until tested."},
{t:"C&E Matrix", x:"Cause and Effect Matrix", p:"Measure", d:"A scoring grid that ranks process inputs by how strongly they affect the outputs the customer cares about. Outputs are weighted by importance, inputs are scored against each, and the products are summed. Turns a long unranked input list into a shortlist worth measuring."},
{t:"FMEA", x:"Failure Mode and Effects Analysis", p:"Measure", d:"A structured review of how each process step could fail, what the consequence would be, how often it happens and whether you would catch it. Produces a risk priority number for each failure mode so attention goes to the worst risks first. A living document, updated as the project learns."},
{t:"RPN", x:"Risk Priority Number", p:"Measure", d:"Severity multiplied by occurrence multiplied by detection, each rated 1 to 10, giving a score from 1 to 1000. Used to rank failure modes. Treat it as a sorting aid rather than an absolute measure — a severity of 10 deserves attention regardless of what the product comes to."},
{t:"Severity", p:"Measure", d:"How bad the consequence is if the failure reaches the customer, rated 1 to 10. The one FMEA rating you usually cannot reduce without redesigning the product or process itself."},
{t:"Occurrence", p:"Measure", d:"How often the failure mode is expected to happen, rated 1 to 10. Reduced by addressing the cause — which is what the Improve phase does."},
{t:"Detection", p:"Measure", d:"How likely you are to catch the failure before it reaches the customer, rated 1 to 10, where 10 means you almost certainly would not. Improved by better controls, though error-proofing the cause is always preferable to detecting it afterwards."},
{t:"MSA", x:"Measurement System Analysis", p:"Measure", d:"Checking that your measurement system is trustworthy before you trust the data it produces. Covers accuracy, precision, bias, linearity and stability. Skipping it risks spending the whole project chasing variation that exists only in the measuring."},
{t:"Gage R&R", x:"Gage Repeatability and Reproducibility", p:"Measure", d:"A study that separates measurement variation into equipment variation (repeatability) and operator variation (reproducibility). Under 10% of total variation is acceptable, 10–30% is marginal, over 30% means the measurement system needs fixing first."},
{t:"Repeatability", p:"Measure", d:"Variation when the same person measures the same item repeatedly with the same instrument. Points at the instrument or the method rather than the people."},
{t:"Reproducibility", p:"Measure", d:"Variation when different people measure the same item with the same instrument. Points at inconsistent technique, unclear definitions or inadequate training."},
{t:"Bias", p:"Measure", d:"A consistent offset between measured values and the true value — every reading two units high, for instance. Correctable by calibration once identified."},
{t:"Linearity", p:"Measure", d:"Whether bias stays constant across the measurement range. A scale that reads accurately at low weights and drifts at high ones has a linearity problem."},
{t:"Stability", p:"Measure", d:"Whether the measurement system holds its performance over time. Assessed by measuring a known reference repeatedly across days or weeks and plotting the results on a control chart."},
{t:"Continuous Data", p:"Measure", d:"Data that can take any value on a scale — time, weight, cost, temperature. Far more informative per observation than discrete data, so it needs much smaller samples. Prefer it wherever the choice exists."},
{t:"Discrete Data", p:"Measure", d:"Also called attribute data. Counts and categories: pass or fail, number of errors, defect type. Easy to collect and much less informative, requiring large samples to detect anything."},
{t:"Mean", p:"Measure", d:"The arithmetic average. Sensitive to outliers, which is why it is reported alongside the median when the distribution is skewed."},
{t:"Median", p:"Measure", d:"The middle value when the data is ordered. Unaffected by extreme values, making it the better centre measure for skewed data such as cycle times."},
{t:"Standard Deviation", p:"Measure", d:"The typical distance of observations from the mean, and the fundamental measure of variation in Six Sigma. The sigma in the name refers to it. Nearly every capability and control calculation is built on it."},
{t:"Normal Distribution", p:"Measure", d:"The symmetric bell-shaped distribution where about 68% of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean and 99.7% within three. Many statistical tests assume it, so normality is checked before those tests are applied."},
{t:"Process Capability", p:"Measure", d:"How well the process fits within its specification limits. Assessed only after the process is shown to be stable — capability figures from an unstable process describe a moment, not the process."},
{t:"Cp", p:"Measure", d:"Potential capability: the specification width divided by six standard deviations. Ignores where the process is centred, so it tells you whether the process could fit if it were perfectly aimed."},
{t:"Cpk", p:"Measure", d:"Actual capability, accounting for centring. Always equal to or lower than Cp; a large gap between them means the process is off-centre and needs re-aiming rather than tightening. 1.33 is a common minimum expectation."},
{t:"Pp and Ppk", p:"Measure", d:"Performance indices, calculated the same way as Cp and Cpk but using long-term overall variation instead of short-term within-subgroup variation. The gap between Cpk and Ppk indicates how much the process drifts over time."},
{t:"USL and LSL", x:"Upper and Lower Specification Limit", p:"Measure", d:"The boundaries of acceptable output, set by the customer or by requirement. Never confuse these with control limits, which come from the process itself — specification limits say what is wanted, control limits say what the process actually delivers."},
{t:"X-Y Diagram", p:"Measure", d:"Another name for the cause and effect matrix, emphasising that it maps the x's (inputs) against the Y's (outputs). Same tool, same scoring logic."},
{t:"Accuracy", p:"Measure", d:"How close measurements sit to the true value on average. A system can be accurate on average while being wildly imprecise, which is why both are assessed."},
{t:"Precision", p:"Measure", d:"How tightly repeated measurements cluster together, regardless of whether they cluster around the right value. Repeatability and reproducibility are its two components."},
{t:"Population", p:"Measure", d:"Every item in the group you want to draw conclusions about. Rarely measurable in full, which is why sampling and inferential statistics exist."},
{t:"Sample", p:"Measure", d:"A subset of the population, selected so that conclusions drawn from it apply to the whole. How the sample is chosen matters more than how large it is — a big biased sample is worse than a small random one."},
{t:"Mode", p:"Measure", d:"The most frequently occurring value. Most useful with discrete data, or for spotting that a distribution has two peaks and is therefore probably two processes mixed together."},
{t:"Range", p:"Measure", d:"The largest value minus the smallest. Crude but easy, and still used inside control chart calculations where subgroups are small."},
{t:"Variance", p:"Measure", d:"The standard deviation squared. Less intuitive to read because the units are squared, but it adds cleanly across independent sources, which makes it the working unit in ANOVA and Gage R&R."},
{t:"Skewness", p:"Measure", d:"How asymmetric a distribution is. Cycle times are typically right-skewed — a floor at zero and a long tail of slow cases. Skewed data breaks the assumptions behind t-tests and ANOVA."},
{t:"Kurtosis", p:"Measure", d:"How heavy the tails of a distribution are relative to a normal curve. High kurtosis means extreme values occur more often than a normal distribution predicts, which matters when estimating defect rates."},
{t:"Histogram", p:"Measure", d:"A bar chart of how frequently values fall into each range. The fastest way to see the shape, centre and spread of data, and to spot two processes masquerading as one."},
{t:"Box Plot", p:"Measure", d:"A compact display of median, quartiles and outliers. Excellent for comparing several groups side by side — shifts and spread differences show up immediately."},
{t:"Run Chart", p:"Measure", d:"Data plotted in time order with a median line. Reveals trends, shifts and cycles that summary statistics hide. A control chart is a run chart with statistically calculated limits added."},
{t:"Scatter Plot", p:"Measure", d:"Two variables plotted against each other to reveal whether a relationship exists. The starting point for correlation and regression, and a reminder that relationships can be strong without being linear."},

/* ---------- ANALYZE ---------- */
{t:"Hypothesis Testing", p:"Analyze", d:"A formal procedure for deciding whether an observed difference is real or could plausibly be chance. State a null hypothesis of no difference, collect data, and calculate how likely that data would be if the null were true. The backbone of the Analyze phase."},
{t:"Null Hypothesis", x:"H0", p:"Analyze", d:"The default assumption that there is no difference, no effect, no relationship. You either reject it or fail to reject it — you never prove it true. Failing to reject means the evidence was insufficient, not that the null is correct."},
{t:"Alternative Hypothesis", x:"Ha", p:"Analyze", d:"The claim that a difference or effect does exist. What you are hoping to demonstrate. Can be one-sided (greater than) or two-sided (different from), which affects the test and the sample size needed."},
{t:"p-value", p:"Analyze", d:"The probability of seeing data at least as extreme as yours if the null hypothesis were true. Below the alpha threshold, usually 0.05, you reject the null. It is not the probability that the null is true, and it says nothing about whether the difference is large enough to matter."},
{t:"Alpha Risk", p:"Analyze", d:"Also called Type I error or producer's risk. The chance of concluding there is an effect when there is not — a false alarm. Set before testing, conventionally at 0.05, meaning you accept a 5% chance of being wrong in that direction."},
{t:"Beta Risk", p:"Analyze", d:"Also called Type II error or consumer's risk. The chance of missing a real effect. Usually set at 0.10 or 0.20. Reduced mainly by increasing sample size, which is why power and sample size calculations exist."},
{t:"Power", p:"Analyze", d:"The probability of detecting an effect that genuinely exists, equal to one minus beta. Typically targeted at 80% or better. An underpowered study that finds nothing has told you nothing."},
{t:"t-Test", p:"Analyze", d:"Compares means. One-sample tests a mean against a target; two-sample compares two independent groups; paired compares before and after on the same units. Assumes roughly normal data and, for the two-sample version, similar variances."},
{t:"ANOVA", x:"Analysis of Variance", p:"Analyze", d:"Compares the means of three or more groups at once. Running multiple t-tests instead inflates the alpha risk with every comparison, which is precisely the problem ANOVA solves. A significant result says at least one group differs, not which."},
{t:"Chi-Squared Test", p:"Analyze", d:"Tests whether two categorical variables are related, using a contingency table of observed versus expected counts. The standard tool when both your Y and your x are attribute data."},
{t:"Correlation", p:"Analyze", d:"The strength and direction of a linear relationship between two continuous variables, measured by r from -1 to +1. Correlation does not establish causation, and a coefficient near zero rules out only a linear relationship."},
{t:"R-Squared", p:"Analyze", d:"The proportion of variation in the output explained by the inputs in the model, from 0 to 1. Directly relevant here — the Black Belt project requires inputs explaining at least 50% of the variation in Y."},
{t:"Regression", p:"Analyze", d:"Fits an equation describing how the output changes as inputs change, giving you a usable model of Y = f(x). Simple regression uses one input, multiple regression several. The residuals must be checked before the model is trusted."},
{t:"Non-Parametric Tests", p:"Analyze", d:"Tests that make no assumption about the distribution, used when data is clearly non-normal and cannot be transformed. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis, Mood's median and the sign test are the common ones. Less powerful than their parametric equivalents, so they need more data."},
{t:"Confidence Interval", p:"Analyze", d:"A range of plausible values for the true parameter, given the data. Often more useful than a p-value because it shows the size of the effect and the precision of the estimate, not just whether it cleared a threshold."},
{t:"CLT", x:"Central Limit Theorem", p:"Analyze", d:"The result that sample means tend toward a normal distribution as sample size grows, regardless of the shape of the underlying population. This is why tests that assume normality still work on non-normal data when samples are reasonably large."},
{t:"Multi-Vari Analysis", p:"Analyze", d:"A graphical technique for seeing where variation lives — within a unit, between units, or over time — before formal testing begins. Narrows the search for causes cheaply and often prevents a wasted round of experiments."},
{t:"Delta", p:"Analyze", d:"The difference between means that you want the test to be able to detect, also called the effect. The ratio of delta to sigma drives sample size: a small ratio means the distributions overlap heavily and you need many samples to separate them."},
{t:"Delta/Sigma Ratio", p:"Analyze", d:"The effect size relative to the process standard deviation. A high ratio means differences are easy to detect with few samples; a low ratio means you need a lot of data. The single most useful number when planning how much data to collect."},
{t:"Power Curve", p:"Analyze", d:"A plot showing how the power of a test changes as the difference you are trying to detect changes. Makes the trade-off visible: smaller differences require larger samples for the same power."},
{t:"Multimodal Distribution", p:"Analyze", d:"Data with more than one peak, almost always a sign that two or more distinct processes have been combined into one data set. The fix is stratification — split the data at the gaps and analyse the subsets separately."},
{t:"Stratification", p:"Analyze", d:"Splitting mixed data into meaningful subgroups by source, shift, operator or product. Often turns non-normal data into several normal data sets, each with a smaller standard deviation and a clearer story."},
{t:"Individual Distribution Identification", p:"Analyze", d:"A Minitab tool that tests data against many distributions and transformations at once, reporting a p-value for each. Used when data fails normality and you need to know what shape it actually has. The course warns against reaching for transformations without expert guidance."},
{t:"Mann-Whitney Test", p:"Analyze", d:"The non-parametric equivalent of the 2-sample t-test, comparing two unpaired samples using ranks. Null hypothesis is that the two samples are equal."},
{t:"Kruskal-Wallis Test", p:"Analyze", d:"The non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA, comparing medians across three or more groups. Requires the data to be stacked into a single response column with a grouping column."},
{t:"1-Sample Wilcoxon", p:"Analyze", d:"The non-parametric equivalent of the 1-sample t-test, comparing a sample median against a target value. Assumes the distribution is roughly symmetric about the median."},
{t:"Sign Test", p:"Analyze", d:"The simplest non-parametric test, checking whether there are more positive than negative differences than chance would produce. Makes very few assumptions and has correspondingly little power."},

/* ---------- IMPROVE ---------- */
{t:"DOE", x:"Design of Experiments", p:"Improve", d:"A structured method for changing several inputs at once according to a plan, so you can measure their individual effects and their interactions with far fewer runs than testing one factor at a time. The most powerful tool in the Improve phase and the hardest to learn."},
{t:"Factor", p:"Improve", d:"An input variable deliberately changed during an experiment. Chosen from the shortlist that survived the C&E matrix and the Analyze phase."},
{t:"Main Effect", p:"Improve", d:"The average change in the output caused by moving one factor from its low level to its high level, across all runs. The headline result of a factorial experiment."},
{t:"Interaction", p:"Improve", d:"When the effect of one factor depends on the level of another. Interactions are invisible to one-factor-at-a-time testing, which is the central argument for running designed experiments instead."},
{t:"Full Factorial", p:"Improve", d:"An experiment running every combination of factor levels — 2 to the power k for k factors at two levels. Gives clean estimates of all main effects and interactions, at the cost of run count growing fast."},
{t:"Fractional Factorial", p:"Improve", d:"A carefully chosen subset of a full factorial, used when the full design is too expensive. Buys the reduction by confounding some effects with others, so you must know which ones before you interpret the results."},
{t:"Confounding", p:"Improve", d:"Also called aliasing. When two effects cannot be separated because the design does not distinguish them — a main effect might be tangled with a two-factor interaction. Managed by choosing an appropriate resolution."},
{t:"Resolution", p:"Improve", d:"How badly a fractional design confounds its effects, written as a Roman numeral. Resolution III confounds main effects with two-factor interactions, IV keeps main effects clear but tangles the two-factor interactions, V keeps both clear. Higher resolution costs more runs."},
{t:"Center Points", p:"Improve", d:"Runs at the midpoint of every factor, added to a two-level design. They test whether the relationship is curved rather than straight and provide an estimate of pure experimental error at no extra design complexity."},
{t:"Level", p:"Improve", d:"A specific setting a factor takes during the experiment — usually a high and a low value. Set them far enough apart to produce a detectable effect but within the range the process can actually run."},
{t:"Residual", p:"Improve", d:"The difference between an observed value and the value the model predicts. Residuals should look like random noise; patterns in them mean the model is missing something and its conclusions are unsafe."},
{t:"OFAT", x:"One-Factor-at-a-Time", p:"Improve", d:"Changing one input while holding the rest constant. Traditional, intuitive, and inefficient — it takes more runs than a factorial design to reach the same conclusion and it cannot detect interactions at all. The central argument for DOE."},
{t:"KPIV", x:"Key Process Input Variable", p:"Improve", d:"An input deliberately manipulated or controlled in an experiment. Identified from the C&E matrix, FMEA, process mapping, multi-vari work and operator knowledge — not from guesswork."},
{t:"KPOV", x:"Key Process Output Variable", p:"Improve", d:"The response you measure in an experiment. Continuous data gives far better DOE results than attribute data, so attribute responses are often converted using a rating scale."},
{t:"Design Matrix", p:"Improve", d:"The table of experimental runs, each row specifying the setting of every factor for that run. Sometimes called the treatment recipe. It is both the plan for running the experiment and the structure the analysis depends on."},
{t:"Corner Point", p:"Improve", d:"A run at a combination of the extreme high and low settings of every factor. In a two-level design the corner points are the whole design, geometrically the vertices of a cube."},
{t:"Replicate", p:"Improve", d:"Running the entire experiment again with fresh randomisation. Gives a genuine estimate of experimental error and increases confidence. Distinct from repeated measurements, which measure the same run twice and produce a weaker result."},
{t:"Repeated Measurement", p:"Improve", d:"Taking several measurements from a single experimental run rather than repeating the run. Averaged for analysis, but less informative than a true replicate because it captures only measurement variation, not process variation."},
{t:"Randomisation", p:"Improve", d:"Running the experiment in random order so that unknown time-related effects — tool wear, warm-up, operator fatigue — are spread across all treatments rather than confounded with one factor. Essential in real experiments; the course sometimes switches it off in teaching exercises for clarity."},
{t:"Screening Experiment", p:"Improve", d:"An early experiment whose only purpose is to find which of many candidate inputs actually matter. Usually a low-resolution fractional design run with a large factor count and accepted confounding."},
{t:"Characterisation Experiment", p:"Improve", d:"An experiment run to develop a predictive equation once the important factors are known. Usually a full factorial, since clean estimates of interactions matter here."},
{t:"Optimisation Experiment", p:"Improve", d:"The final stage, estimating curvature and interactions well enough to find the best settings. Response surface designs and centre points belong to this phase."},
{t:"Response Surface Methodology", p:"Improve", d:"A family of designs that model curvature in the response, used when a straight-line model is not good enough and you need to locate an optimum rather than just a direction."},
{t:"Path of Steepest Ascent", p:"Improve", d:"The direction in factor space along which the response improves fastest, worked out from an initial experiment and used to choose where to run the next one. Turns DOE into a sequence rather than a single event."},
{t:"Projection", p:"Improve", d:"The property that a fractional factorial collapses into a stronger design in fewer factors if some of the original factors turn out not to matter. Part of why accepting confounding up front is a reasonable bet."},
{t:"Sequential Experimentation", p:"Improve", d:"Running a series of smaller experiments, each informed by the last, rather than one large one. The course's rule of thumb is to spend under 25% of the budget on the first experiment."},
{t:"Response Optimizer", p:"Improve", d:"A Minitab tool that works the regression equation backwards: you state a target for Y and it returns the factor settings that hit it, subject to any constraints you set. Where DOE stops being analysis and becomes a design tool."},
{t:"Desirability", p:"Improve", d:"A score from 0 to 1 reporting how closely a set of factor settings meets your stated goal, shown as 'd' in the optimizer. A d of 1.0 means the model predicts an exact match to target. Composite desirability combines multiple responses into one score."},
{t:"Contour Plot", p:"Improve", d:"A map of the response across two factors, with contour bands showing where the output lands. Overlaid contour plots add the customer's requirement range, and the white zone that remains shows every factor combination that satisfies it."},
{t:"Prediction Interval", p:"Improve", d:"The range within which a single future observation is expected to fall. Wider than a confidence interval, which covers the average rather than an individual — the course flags mixing these up as a common error."},
{t:"Lack-of-Fit", p:"Improve", d:"A test of whether the model form is adequate. A lack-of-fit p-value above 0.05 is good news: you cannot reject the hypothesis that the model fits. Below 0.05 means the model is missing something, often curvature."},
{t:"R-sq Adjusted", p:"Improve", d:"R-squared penalised for the number of predictors in the model. Always used instead of plain R-squared in multiple regression, because plain R-squared rises whenever you add a term, useful or not."},
{t:"R-sq Predicted", p:"Improve", d:"An estimate of how well the model would predict new observations. If it sits far below R-sq adjusted, the model is overfitted — it has learned the noise in this data set rather than the underlying relationship."},
{t:"VIF", x:"Variance Inflation Factor", p:"Improve", d:"A measure of how much a predictor overlaps with the others. VIF of 1 means fully independent, 1 to 5 moderate correlation, above 5 excessive. All terms in a final model must be at or under 5. Remove high p-value predictors before high VIF ones, one at a time."},
{t:"Least Squares Method", p:"Improve", d:"The method for fitting a regression line: choose the line that minimises the sum of the squared vertical distances between the data points and the line. Those distances are the residuals."},
{t:"Pearson Correlation", p:"Improve", d:"The standard correlation measure for two continuous variables, producing the coefficient r between -1 and +1. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination."},
{t:"Coefficient of Determination", p:"Improve", d:"R-squared — the share of variation in the output explained by the model. Directly relevant to certification: the Analyze phase requires at least 50%."},
{t:"BLR", x:"Binary Logistic Regression", p:"Improve", d:"Regression for a response with exactly two mutually exclusive outcomes — sale or no sale, defect or no defect. Predicts a probability between 0 and 1 rather than a value, and requires neither normality nor a linear relationship."},
{t:"Sigmoid Function", p:"Improve", d:"The S-shaped curve that maps any real number into the range 0 to 1. What makes logistic regression produce probabilities instead of the nonsensical values a straight line would give for binary data."},
{t:"Odds", p:"Improve", d:"The ratio of an outcome occurring to it not occurring — distinct from probability, where the denominator includes all outcomes. Three red balls in nine give a probability of 0.33 but odds of 3 to 6."},
{t:"Odds Ratio", p:"Improve", d:"How much more likely the outcome is at one level of a factor than another. An odds ratio of 3.95 for shoppers with children means they were nearly four times as likely to buy. The headline output of a binary logistic regression."},

/* ---------- CONTROL ---------- */
{t:"SPC", x:"Statistical Process Control", p:"Control", d:"Monitoring a process over time with control charts so that changes are detected as they happen rather than discovered later. The core of the Control phase, and the mechanism that stops the improvement quietly reverting."},
{t:"Control Chart", p:"Control", d:"A time-ordered plot with a centre line and statistically derived control limits. Points inside the limits and randomly scattered indicate a stable process; points outside, or non-random patterns, signal that something has changed. Choosing the right chart depends on data type and subgroup size."},
{t:"Common Cause", p:"Control", d:"The ordinary background variation inherent in a process, present in every observation. Reducing it requires changing the process itself. Reacting to individual common-cause points as if they were problems makes performance worse — this is called tampering."},
{t:"Special Cause", p:"Control", d:"Variation from an identifiable external event — a new supplier, a substitution, an equipment fault. Shows on a control chart as a point outside the limits or a non-random pattern. Worth investigating individually, unlike common cause."},
{t:"UCL and LCL", x:"Upper and Lower Control Limit", p:"Control", d:"The boundaries of expected variation, calculated from the process data itself and usually set at three standard deviations either side of the centre line. Not the same as specification limits — control limits describe what the process does, specifications describe what is wanted."},
{t:"I-MR Chart", x:"Individuals and Moving Range Chart", p:"Control", d:"For continuous data collected one observation at a time, where subgrouping is impossible. The individuals chart tracks the values, the moving range chart tracks the difference between consecutive points. Limits are typically the mean plus or minus 2.66 times the average moving range."},
{t:"Xbar-R Chart", p:"Control", d:"For continuous data in small subgroups, usually between two and nine. The Xbar chart tracks subgroup averages and the R chart tracks subgroup ranges. The most widely used pair in manufacturing."},
{t:"P Chart", p:"Control", d:"For the proportion of defective units, where sample size varies between subgroups. The control limits change width as the sample size changes."},
{t:"U Chart", p:"Control", d:"For defects per unit where the inspection area or sample size varies. The variable-sample-size counterpart of the C chart."},
{t:"Control Plan", p:"Control", d:"The document specifying what gets measured, how often, by whom, with what method, and what to do when it drifts. The formal handover from the project team to the process owner, and the thing that makes an improvement permanent."},
{t:"Response Plan", p:"Control", d:"The part of the control plan that says exactly what happens when a measurement goes out of limits — who is notified, what is checked, what is stopped. Without it, a control chart is just a chart that nobody acts on."},
{t:"Center Line", p:"Control", d:"The average of the plotted statistic on a control chart. The reference against which points and patterns are judged."},
{t:"Subgroup", p:"Control", d:"A small set of consecutive units measured together and plotted as one point. Chosen so that variation within the subgroup reflects only common cause, which is what makes the control limits meaningful."},
{t:"Xbar-S Chart", p:"Control", d:"Like Xbar-R but using standard deviation instead of range to measure within-subgroup variation. Preferred when subgroups are larger than about nine, where the range becomes an inefficient estimator."},
{t:"NP Chart", p:"Control", d:"For the count of defective units when the sample size is constant. Simpler to read than a P chart because the limits stay flat."},
{t:"C Chart", p:"Control", d:"For the count of defects — not defectives — per unit, where the inspection area is constant. Multiple defects on one unit are all counted."},
{t:"CuSum Chart", x:"Cumulative Sum Chart", p:"Control", d:"Plots the accumulated deviation from a target rather than individual values. Far more sensitive than a standard chart to small sustained shifts, at the cost of responding more slowly to large sudden ones."},
{t:"EWMA Chart", x:"Exponentially Weighted Moving Average Chart", p:"Control", d:"Plots a weighted average that gives most weight to recent observations and progressively less to older ones. Like CuSum, it is tuned for detecting small persistent shifts rather than dramatic ones."},
{t:"Reaction Plan", p:"Control", d:"The part of the control plan specifying what happens when a measurement goes out of limits: the steps, a timeframe for resolution, who owns each action, and pre-approved resources. The course compares it to a fire drill."},
{t:"Communication Plan", p:"Control", d:"A documented plan covering who needs to hear what, from whom, by what method and when — with a feedback route built in. Its stated purpose is to kill rumours before they set."},
{t:"Cadence of Accountability", p:"Control", d:"Regular scheduled check-ins on the improvement after implementation. The mechanism that stops gains quietly eroding once the project team disbands."},
{t:"Change Effectiveness Formula", p:"Control", d:"Effectiveness equals Quality multiplied by Acceptance. A technically excellent solution nobody accepts scores zero. The course's argument for spending real effort on the human side of implementation."},
{t:"Stakeholder Analysis", p:"Control", d:"Mapping stakeholders on interest or impact against influence, then adding an opposition rating to produce a risk priority number. Identifies who needs attention and who can be leveraged, including low-influence people who can still shift opinion."},
{t:"Six Thinking Hats", p:"Control", d:"De Bono's technique for forcing a group to examine a decision from six angles in turn: White for facts, Red for feelings, Black for flaws, Yellow for benefits, Green for new ideas, Blue for managing the process."},
{t:"Thumb Voting", p:"Control", d:"A fast consensus check where everyone signals up, down or sideways. Thumbs to the side is the useful signal — it means more discussion is needed, not necessarily disagreement. Designed to surface concerns rather than suppress them, and to guard against groupthink."}

];
